Compile. Include symbolic debugging information.
gcc -g file.c <-o execfile>
main: ELF 64-bit LSB pie executable, x86-64, version 1 (SYSV), dynamically linked, interpreter /lib64/ld-linux-x86-64.so.2, BuildID[sha1]=895926d15b9596490cb43c307449f377e42ae5dd, for GNU/Linux 4.4.0, (with debug_info), not stripped

Start in GUI mode., arrow keys, pgup/down work.
gdb -tui execfile

GNU GDB front-end
DDD - DisplayDataDebugger.

Commands
help - get help.
apropos keyword - for help on "keyword"
quit/q - quit the program.
return - last executed command.
run/r <args list> - to start executing the program
list sourcefile:foo - to bring about line foo in the sourcefile on the source window.
(file exec) followed by run <arglist> - Loads exec and then runs it against arglist.


-- Breakpoints.
break main	Break at the beginning of the main() function
break 5	Break at line 5 of the current file i.e line 5 will be the next to be executed.
break hello.c:5	Break at line 5 of hello.c
info breakpoints/i b - Get info about the breakpoints.
enable breakpointnum - Not the line number, but the breakpoint number. {enb tab(y/n)}
delete <breakpointnum1 bpn2 ..>
clear pointname,lnum - delete a breakpoint by the line number, name etc.

-- Stepping around
next/n - Move to the next line.
step/s - Step into a subroutine.
continue/c - Go ahead until you encounter the next break point.
    C-c to stop exec in the current place, get to a gdb prompt and then set breakpoints, the c debugging.

-- Inspecting Variables.
display/disp varname -- Displays the current value of variable, if in scope (useful during loops)
info display - Get the displaynum for all the set variables.

    (gdb) disp i
    1: i = -1207447872
    ^__ displaynum

undisplay displaynumofvar
print var - know the value one-of(one time)
printf formatted_string vars - for formatted printing.
    printf "%d\n", i

-- Window
ref - to refresh the GUI window, clears all the mess.

-- Misc
bt/backtrace - To look at the current stack trace.
finish - Exit the current function and return to the caller.
stepi - Step over a single asm instruction.Complete the until the end of the current function.
advance func - advance from current location to func() call.
jump loc - program control to loc. If you wanna stop here, set a breakpoint here.
x/i $pc - extract instruction at PC

-- Changing variables, values at runtime.
    set (i = 20)
    set variable i = 20 -- Can be used with jump to repeat sections of code without restarting.

-- Hardware Watchpoints, look for the changes in the value an expression evaluates to.
info watch
watch var - watch var, watch a[10], watch *(p+10)
rwatch - detect when the variable is read.
awatch - detect when the variable is either read/written.

-- Attaching to a running process.
gdb
attach pid

-- Core dumped, SIGSEGV
$gdb -c corefilepath
This means that a core file (with a memory snapshot from the time of the crash) has been created with the name "core". If you're not getting a core file (that is, it only says "Segmentation fault" and no core file is created), you might have your ulimit set too low; try typing ulimit -c unlimited at your shell prompt.

$gdb -tui -c core foo
    If in TUI mode, you'll be greeted with a screen of information, telling you why the program exited ("signal 11, Segmentation fault"), and the highlight will be on the offending line. (In dumb terminal mode, the offending line is printed out.)
